    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Stand Up! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Stand Up!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can trace letters of the alphabet and simple familiar words with teacher guidance (e.g., tracing dotted lines to form alphabet letters and familiar words, following teacher guidance).
Can engage in interactive labeling activities by matching words to labeled classroom objects (e.g., 'pen' or 'book').
Can copy simple familiar words to label drawings and pictures (e.g., 'sit' or 'pen').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a TPR activity. "Good morning, students! Please stand up!" (Teacher models 'stand up'). "Please listen." (Teacher points to ear). "Touch the book." (Teacher touches a book). "Please, sit." (Teacher sits). The teacher uses flashcards of classroom objects (chair, desk, book, window, door) and points to them, asking "What is it?" for oral review. (Embedding: Imperatives, Nouns, Pragmatic - Following instructions, Responding questions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a picture of a book. "It is a book." (Teacher pronounces /b/ in 'book' clearly). "This is the word 'book'." The teacher writes "book" clearly on the whiteboard, showing the letter formation. Then, the teacher shows a worksheet with a dotted line word 'book' and demonstrates tracing it with a finger, explaining, "We trace the letters." The teacher highlights initial sounds, e.g., /t/ for 'touch'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a chair): "Is this a book?" (Expected answer: "No").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'book' written on the board): "What is this word?" (Expected answer: "Book").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful classroom poster depicting a 'school day' scene. The poster features common classroom objects: a 'chair', 'desk', 'book', 'window', and 'door', each clearly labeled with its corresponding word printed in a clear, easy-to-read font below the image. The poster also includes small children (students) and a teacher. Some labels could have dotted lines for emphasis. (Genre: instructional chart, Register: simple, informal, Format: visual poster with text).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing actions: Using basic words to give an instruction, like 'Please, sit.' Responding questions: Answering WH-questions like 'What is it?'. (Embedding: Present simple 'It is a school', 'It is a book', Nouns: teacher, student, chair, desk, book, window, door).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture on the poster (e.g., a chair) and asks, "What is it?" Students respond chorally, "It is a chair." (Teacher stresses 'teacher' and 'student' syllables).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a word (e.g., "book") and asks students to point to the corresponding picture and word on the poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Where is the window?" Students point to the window on the poster and to the word 'window'. (Embedding: Prepositions: near).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice tracing and copying simple familiar words, focusing on correct letter formation and word recognition. (Grammar: Present simple 'It is a...', Vocabulary: chair, desk, book, window, door).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Tracing Activity (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with large pictures of a 'chair', 'desk', 'book', 'window', and 'door'. Below each picture, the corresponding word is written in dotted lines. Students use pencils or crayons to trace the letters of each word. The teacher walks around, providing individual guidance on tracing and pronouncing the words. (Embedding: Pronunciation /s/ in 'school', /l/ in 'listen', initial sound /b/ 'book', /t/ 'touch').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Match and Trace (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students receive another worksheet with pictures of the five classroom objects on the left and the words ('chair', 'desk', 'book', 'window', 'door') mixed up on the right. Students draw a line from each picture to its correct word. Then, they trace the matched word. The teacher reinforces by saying, "This is a desk. Match 'desk'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Copying Words (Guided Practice/Peer Check):</b> The teacher displays flashcards with the words 'chair', 'desk', 'book', 'window', 'door' clearly written. Students receive a worksheet with small blank spaces below images of these objects. They copy the correct word from the flashcard onto their worksheet to label each picture. Students then check their neighbor's worksheet, pointing to any word they think is incorrect and saying, "Check 'book'." (Embedding: Sociolinguistic - Politeness and formality in instructions).</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written product - "My Classroom Labeling."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Task:</b> Each student receives a simplified, outline drawing of a classroom scene for "It's a School Day!". The drawing includes a 'desk', a 'chair', a 'window', a 'door', and a 'book' on the desk, but without labels. The teacher provides a word bank on the board with the words: 'chair', 'desk', 'book', 'window', 'door'. Students are instructed to copy the correct word from the word bank to label each corresponding object in their drawing. They can color the picture after labeling. The teacher reminds them, "Please copy the word carefully, just like you traced it. This is your school, and you are labeling it!" (Embedding: Nouns, Pragmatic - Following instructions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in copying words, collaborative interaction (if students help each other identify objects), and communicative task completion (successfully labeling the classroom drawing).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the labeling activity and review of the completed "My Classroom Labeling" drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their labeled classroom drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly matches at least 4 out of 5 words to the corresponding objects in the drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Copies the target words ('chair', 'desk', 'book', 'window', 'door') with recognizable letter formation (legibility).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to label all provided objects.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up their drawings. The teacher asks: "Did you try your best to write the words?" (Students show thumbs up/down). "Can you find the word 'book' on your paper?" (Students point to it). (Embedding: Adjectives: nice).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which word was easy to write?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson for Kinder students? Were the tracing and copying activities appropriate for their fine motor skills and CEFR level? What adjustments are needed for the next writing activity? Do students need more guided practice with letter formation, or are they ready for slightly more complex words or short phrases? The visuals and TPR were very helpful. Next time, I will ensure students have individual word banks to help with copying.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The labeled classroom objects and the ability to recognize their written forms will be crucial for the Lesson 5 Mediation & Integration task, where students might describe their classroom or create a simple classroom map with labels.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>